---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const pillars = [
  {
    id: 'community',
    number: '01',
    title: 'Built with the Strix Halo community',
    subtitle: 'One stop shop for Strix Halo local AI enthusiasts',
    content: [
      'Contributions are welcome. Gufo needs the help of the Strix Halo community to keep improving, and the project is run so that an outside change can be measured and reviewed the same way an internal one is.',
      'The goal is batteries included: text, audio, image and video models in one executable with one OpenAI-compatible server, rather than a text engine plus a pile of separate Python stacks for everything else.',
      'The repository carries what a contributor needs to verify a change: per-model benchmark tables with their scope and date, per-model quality reports with their unresolved gaps, and a short record of retained and rejected experiments.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'single-target',
    number: '02',
    title: 'Build and optimize for one machine',
    subtitle: 'Strix Halo with 128 GiB of unified memory is the design point',
    content: [
      'Engines that target CUDA, Metal, oneAPI and generic CPU at once pay for it in leaky abstractions, indirect dispatch and memory layouts chosen by the lowest common denominator. Gufo takes the opposite trade: Linux x86-64 on AMD Strix Halo is the only planned production target, and the HIP backend accepts gfx1151 alone.',
      'Smaller memory configurations still work and preserve the speed benefits for models that fit; the optimization work simply assumes the 128 GiB machine when choosing what to support.',
      'Unified LPDDR5X memory shared by CPU and GPU is what makes a 27B dense model, an 80.76 GiB mixture-of-experts graph and the audio models usable on the same desktop-class system.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'curation',
    number: '03',
    title: 'Support only the best models for their size',
    subtitle: 'Less code to maintain, more focused optimization and testing',
    content: [
      'Gufo supports the best available models that run on this hardware, not every model that exists. Fewer families means less code to maintain and more optimization and testing effort per family.',
      'Each model family owns its execution pipeline under src/models/, building as its own static library with its own config, loader, engine, kernels and CPU reference. Kernels are deliberately not reused across models, which limits the blast radius of any change.',
      'Model code, tests, tools and numerical contracts stay with their model, and so do their four maintained documents: usage, benchmarks, quality and experiments.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'quality',
    number: '04',
    title: 'Preserve quality when optimizing',
    subtitle: 'Each model records independent numerical checks and unresolved gaps',
    content: [
      'A quality report distinguishes agreement with an independent model implementation from consistency between Gufo execution modes. Greedy speculative decoding should reproduce autoregressive output; sampled speculation can use different random draws, and seeded replay is checked within one execution configuration.',
      'Comparison is matched-token and per position: tokenize once, feed teacher and candidate the same predetermined history, and compare full next-token distributions before appending the next token. Free-running text cannot isolate numerical error after the first differing choice.',
      'Numbers that are not measured yet stay TODO, and comparisons a reference engine cannot run stay N/A with a reason. A numerical mismatch or a device failure is not a passing result, and a skip caused by a missing model is not a quality pass.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'measurement',
    number: '05',
    title: 'Measure the whole workload, then promote',
    subtitle: 'A faster kernel that loses end-to-end time is a rejected candidate',
    content: [
      'The optimization loop is fixed: define the numerical and mutable-state contract, measure the complete workload, classify the dominant phase as bandwidth, compute, launch, synchronization or scheduling limited, build a focused reproducer with a trusted oracle, then change one mechanism at a time.',
      'Comparable runs share source revision, model artifact, quantization, prompt and generation lengths, depth, concurrency, GPU route, driver and ROCm version, and warmup policy. Headline latency comes from an unprofiled run, because tracing changes timing.',
      'A faster isolated kernel is not a win if packing, synchronization or state management erase the gain. Successful optimizations become the default path, without extra switches to select them.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'operations',
    number: '06',
    title: 'Concurrency and reproducibility are features',
    subtitle: 'Concurrent requests, cancellation and caching are first-class workloads',
    content: [
      'Concurrent requests, cancellation and conversation caching are treated as first-class workloads rather than afterthoughts, and they are benchmarked as such: concurrency tables sum individual request decode rates and keep prefill and queue time in separate latency diagnostics.',
      'Production dependencies stay small and development tools stay separate. Tests, profilers, tuning executables and Python reference runners are not installed with the production package, and Python is never required to serve a request.',
      'CMake owns the build for both Nix and ordinary Linux; Nix pins the complete toolchain so comparisons are reproducible. For users, the companion toolboxes project ships images that run as a dedicated non-root user with no sudo or PAM stack.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Philosophy · Gufo">
  <Navbar currentPath="/philosophy" />

  <main class="flex-1">
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 pt-16 sm:pt-24 pb-24">
      <header class="pb-12 border-b border-line">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-5">Philosophy</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-6xl text-ink leading-[1.08]">Why Gufo does less, on purpose</h1>
        <p class="mt-8 font-serif text-xl text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          Gufo narrows its scope to one machine and a curated model set. Measurement and quality rules decide what gets
          promoted into the default path.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-ink-3">
          Restated from the repository's <code>README.md</code>, <code>docs/PERFORMANCE.md</code> and
          <code>docs/BENCHMARKS.md</code>.
        </p>
      </header>

      <nav class="py-10 border-b border-line" aria-label="Principles">
        <ol class="space-y-2 text-[15px]">
          {pillars.map((p) => (
            <li class="flex gap-4">
              <span class="font-mono text-sm text-ink-4 pt-0.5">{p.number}</span>
              <a href={`#${p.id}`} class="text-ink-2 hover:text-ink underline decoration-transparent hover:decoration-line-strong">
                {p.title}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </nav>

      {pillars.map((p) => (
        <section id={p.id} class="pt-14 scroll-mt-20">
          <p class="eyebrow">{p.number} / Principle</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-3xl text-ink leading-tight">{p.title}</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-ink-3">{p.subtitle}</p>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 font-serif text-[19px] text-ink-2 leading-[1.7]">
            {p.content.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
          </div>
        </section>
      ))}

      <section class="mt-16 pt-10 border-t border-line">
        <h2 class="text-2xl text-ink">In short</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-[15px] text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          <li><span class="text-ink font-medium">One platform.</span> Linux x86-64 on AMD Strix Halo (<code>gfx1151</code>).</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink font-medium">Curated models.</span> Only the best weights for their size; each model owns its compute graph.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink font-medium">Measured claims.</span> Retained numbers carry their scope and date; pending work stays TODO.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink font-medium">Quality first.</span> Optimization never trades away a model's numerical contract.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink font-medium">Reproducible.</span> Nix pins the toolchain, CMake owns the build, containers run non-root.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
